ecc_agent_script_include.script:
ms.include("IllumioHttpIncludes");

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TABLE AND FIELD NAMES BELOW ARE A SECOND COPY, ON PURPOSE.
//
// The centralized copy lives on the instance, in IllumioConstants (TABLE_* globals) and in the
// per-class constants that read from it. A MID script include runs in the MID Server's own
// process and cannot see a scoped Script Include or call gs, so there is no way for it to read
// the instance's copy at parse time. Passing the names down in the ECC message was considered and
// rejected: it would make every probe's parameter list carry them and couple the MID to the exact
// message shape.
//
// So: when a table or field is renamed on the instance, BOTH copies have to change. A mismatch
// here does not raise -- the MID queries a table that does not exist, or a column that does not
// exist, and reports a run that found nothing rather than a run that failed. Check this file
// against IllumioConstants whenever a table is renamed.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

var RETRY_CODES = [429];
// Platform REST paths, not app tables. These two have no instance-side counterpart and are not
// duplicated anywhere -- they are how the MID reaches the instance in the first place.
var TABLE_API = "/api/now/table/";

// W5. Where the MID pushes its error and warning lines so they land in Application Logs next to
// the server-side ones. NOT a table -- a scoped Scripted REST resource whose script only calls
// gs.error/gs.warn. It touches no GlideRecord on purpose: the cross-scope 500 that killed
// UpdateLabelDimensionsValue came out of record access, and a sink that cannot fail that way is
// worth more than one that could carry a richer payload.
//
// The path is <namespace>/<api_id>/<relative_path>. Namespace is the scope; api_id and
// relative_path are set on the API record, so THIS STRING AND THAT RECORD MUST AGREE -- a
// mismatch returns 404 on every push and the only symptom is MID lines quietly not arriving.
var MID_LOG_PUSH_API = "/api/x_illu2_illumio/illumio_mid_log/push";
// R13 / 2026-09-29: superseded, kept for the reasoning. This was the buffer's page size. The
// buffer is gone -- lines go to the instance one at a time now. Measured against what the MID
// already spends, the batching was not paying for itself: _updateJobRecord is a GET plus a PUT
// and the sync batch loop calls it five times, so ~10 instance round-trips per batch are already
// in flight. Turning a healthy 100k run's ~101 log lines into 2 POSTs rather than 101 moved
// instance chatter by ~10%. Against that it cost ordering -- a 100-line batch is written inside
// one second, syslog_app_scope's Created column is second-precision, so a flushed batch came back
// shuffled -- plus module state shared across probe runs, lines dropped whole on a failed push,
// and a finally in every probe that a throw past the wrapper would skip.
// var MID_LOG_BUFFER_MAX = 100;

// Ceiling on log pushes per probe run. NOT a page size -- each push carries one line.
//
// This bounds the one case the buffer was genuinely earning its keep. IllumioUpdateWorkloads has
// ~6 _error sites that fire PER WORKLOAD, not per batch, so a 100k run failing on every workload
// would be 100k POSTs unbounded. A fixed ceiling caps that outright instead of dividing it by the
// page size and still leaving it proportional to the failure count.
//
// A healthy run is nowhere near this: one probe-entered line plus one _milestone per payload
// batch, ~101 lines on a 100k sync. Past the ceiling the run's remaining lines stay in agent0.log
// on the MID host, and the last push spends itself saying so.
var MID_LOG_PUSH_MAX = 500;

// Centralized copy: TABLE_SCHEDULED_JOBS in IllumioConstants.
var SCHEDULED_JOB_TABLE = "x_illu2_illumio_illumio_scheduled_jobs";
// Centralized copy: TABLE_PCE_ASYNC_JOBS in IllumioConstants.
var ASYNC_JOB_TABLE = "x_illu2_illumio_illumio_pce_async_jobs";
// Centralized copy: IllumioSyncUtils.AUTOSYNC_PAYLOAD_TABLE / TABLE_AUTOSYNC_PAYLOAD.
var AUTOSYNC_PAYLOAD_TABLE = "x_illu2_illumio_illumio_autosync_payload";
// P0-1. Deletion carries its workloads in payload rows now rather than in the ECC message, the
// same way auto sync already did.
// Centralized copy: IllumioSyncUtils.DELETION_PAYLOAD_TABLE / TABLE_DELETION_PAYLOAD.
var DELETION_PAYLOAD_TABLE = "x_illu2_illumio_illumio_deletion_payload";
// Preview exports. Unlike the two payload tables above these rows carry finished CSV text rather
// than an operation bundle -- the MID appends them to a file in order and never parses them.
// Centralized copy: IllumioSyncUtils.PREVIEW_PAYLOAD_TABLE / TABLE_PREVIEW_PAYLOAD.
var PREVIEW_PAYLOAD_TABLE = "x_illu2_illumio_illumio_preview_payload";
// The reference back to the Scheduled Job, on all three payload tables above.
//
// R33: this is how the MID finds its work. The ECC message used to carry a comma-separated list
// of payload row sys_ids, which became one XML attribute and grew with the run -- ~132 KB at 2M
// workloads, and worse every time the window size dropped. The message now carries jobSysId only
// and the MID queries for the rows.
//
// Centralized copy: IllumioSyncUtils.PAYLOAD_JOB_FIELD. MUST MATCH IT. Of everything duplicated
// in this file this is the one most likely to bite: a wrong table name at least fails visibly on
// the next call, whereas a wrong COLUMN name in an addQuery does not raise at all -- it simply
// stops constraining, so the MID finds either nothing or everything.
var PAYLOAD_JOB_FIELD = "job_id";
// sysparm_limit for the query that finds a job's payload rows. Set explicitly rather than left to
// the Table API's own default, because that default is a platform setting an admin can change and
// a run silently truncated to the first N rows sends part of itself to the PCE and reports
// success. One row is IllumioSyncUtils.PAYLOAD_ROW_SIZE workloads, so this is 20M workloads' worth
// -- far past any real run, which is the point: it is a guard rail, not a page size. The response
// carries sys_ids only, never the payloads.
var PAYLOAD_ROW_QUERY_LIMIT = 20000;
// Attachment API. A platform REST path, not an app table -- no instance-side counterpart. The
// file is POSTed as the raw request body, so this is the /file endpoint rather than /upload,
// which expects multipart form data.
var ATTACHMENT_FILE_API = "/api/now/attachment/file";
// Centralized copy: TABLE_PCE_LABELS_MAPPING in IllumioConstants. ~100k rows in production --
// see the note there before caching anything read out of it.
var PCE_LABELS_MAPPING_TABLE = "x_illu2_illumio_illumio_pce_labels_mapping";
// Retry settings for the MID-side operations that have no PCE Configuration to read them from
// (authentication, async job polling, preview upload). A CALLER-SIDE default, deliberately: the
// operation chooses it explicitly and it is visible at the call site. IllumioHTTPClient does not
// substitute it -- an operation that is handed an incomplete retry configuration runs without
// retrying rather than with numbers nobody entered. All three are greater than zero, which is
// what IllumioHTTPClient._validateRetryParams requires.
var DEFAULT_RETRY_PARAMS = {
    http_retry_count: 5,
    http_retry_interval_increment: 30,
    http_retry_interval_max: 300
};
// Job statuses, centralized so the MID and the instance spell them the same way. The instance
// half is JOB_STATUS_* in IllumioConstants and the two lists must stay identical -- these values
// ARE the choice list on x_illu2_illumio_illumio_scheduled_jobs.job_status, and a typo on either
// side writes a status no query matches rather than failing loudly. That is the failure these
// replace: a status literal is never validated by anything, so a misspelling is invisible until a
// job silently stops being found by the guard that was supposed to stop it.
//
// Declared ABOVE CANCELLED_JOB_STATUSES on purpose -- that array is built from three of them, and
// `var` hoisting would leave it holding three `undefined`s if this block came after it.
var JOB_STATUS_RUNNING = "running";
var JOB_STATUS_COMPLETED = "completed";
var JOB_STATUS_COMPLETED_WITH_ERROR = "completed_with_error";
var JOB_STATUS_PARTIAL_SUCCESS = "partial_success";
var JOB_STATUS_FAILED = "failed";
var JOB_STATUS_CANCELLED = "cancelled";
var JOB_STATUS_CANCELLED_THRESHOLD = "cancelled_threshold_limit_exceeded";
var JOB_STATUS_INVALIDATED = "invalidated";
// PCE ASYNC JOBS -- a DIFFERENT table (ASYNC_JOB_TABLE) with its OWN job_status choice list. Two
// values spell the same as the scheduled-job ones today; they are deliberately not aliased,
// because they are separate lifecycles and a future change to one must not move the other.
// `ready_to_map` is the proof the split is real -- it is the discovery-to-mapping handoff and
// exists only here. Mirrors ASYNC_JOB_STATUS_* in IllumioConstants.
var ASYNC_JOB_STATUS_COMPLETED = "completed";
var ASYNC_JOB_STATUS_FAILED = "failed";
var ASYNC_JOB_STATUS_READY_TO_MAP = "ready_to_map";
// Job statuses that mean "stop, the instance is no longer expecting this work". The MID checks
// this set rather than the single value 'cancelled', because a job can be stopped three different
// ways and only one of them writes that value:
//   cancelled                          -- operator pressed Cancel, or the wall-clock timeout in
//                                         the Illumio Invalidate Running Job script action fired
//   cancelled_threshold_limit_exceeded -- a configured threshold was hit, or the thresholds could
//                                         not be resolved at all (IllumioThresholdLimit throws)
//   invalidated                        -- the job record was superseded
// Matching only 'cancelled' meant a threshold-cancelled job kept pushing to the PCE for the rest
// of the run: the instance had given up on it and the MID had not been told.
//
// Centralized copy: the same set is IllumioSyncRepoUtils' dead-status list minus the statuses that
// mean the job finished on its own. Those are not here on purpose -- a job that reached
// 'completed' while the MID was still working is a different bug and should not be swallowed as
// an orderly cancellation.
var CANCELLED_JOB_STATUSES = [
    JOB_STATUS_CANCELLED,
    JOB_STATUS_CANCELLED_THRESHOLD,
    JOB_STATUS_INVALIDATED
];
// Chunk size used when `glide.jms.batch_size` / `glide.jms.chunk_size` is missing or unparseable.
// SHOULD MATCH the default on BATCH_SIZE_STANDARD in IllumioConstants -- a MID script include has
// no `gs` and cannot read a system property, so the number is duplicated by hand, the same way
// PAYLOAD_JOB_FIELD is. Divergence is not fatal: it only changes the chunk size on a run whose ECC
// message was already malformed. Read by IllumioManageAsyncJobs and IllumioUpdateWorkloads.
var DEFAULT_MID_BATCH_SIZE = 1000;
// Minimum spacing between consecutive calls to the PCE, in milliseconds. Applies to the PCE
// client only, never to the client that talks back to the instance. This is what actually stops
// the 429s: backoff is reactive and only runs after the PCE has already refused a call, and a
// refusal is an error in the log even when the retry that follows succeeds. Label creation is the
// loop that provoked it -- one POST /api/v2/orgs/N/labels/ per missing label, back to back.
//
// 100ms caps the PCE at ~10 calls/second. On a 10k workload sync the labels are created once and
// then found in cache, so the added wall clock is seconds, not minutes.
var MIN_PCE_CALL_INTERVAL_MS = 100;
